16,18,15170,23,16,1124,15136,220,16,16,22,92,284,1124,37018,90,16,18,15170,23,16,1124,15136,220,16,16,22,532,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nGiven that the third term of a geometric sequence is $1053$ and the ninth term is $\\frac{13}{9}$, find the value of the seventh term.\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for the seventh term of the geometric sequence, we start by recalling the general form of the \\(n\\)-th term of a geometric sequence:\n\\[\na_n = ar^{n-1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a\\) is the first term and \\(r\\) is the common ratio.\n\nGiven:\n- The third term \\(a_3 = 1053\\)\n- The ninth term \\(a_9 = \\frac{13}{9}\\)\n\nUsing the general form, we can write:\n\\[\na_3 = ar^2 = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na_9 = ar^8 = \\frac{13}{9}\n\\]\n\nWe now have two equations:\n1. \\(ar^2 = 1053\\)\n2. \\(ar^8 = \\frac{13}{9}\\)\n\nTo find \\(r\\), we divide the second equation by the first equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{ar^8}{ar^2} = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053}\n\\]\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053} = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053\n\\]\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477} = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify \\(\\frac{13}{9477}\\):\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053 = 9 \\times (9 \\times 117) = 81 \\times 117\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{13}{9477} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117}\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6d50ef89898ef015826f0a96e633ca972b61f7eda1619edcb46e03c426d0d42d"},"seconds":51.06194543838501,"seed":539161518989818840,"stop":"eos","text":"Continuing from the interruption:\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{13}{9477}\\), we note that:\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053 = 9 \\times (9 \\times 117) = 81 \\times 117\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\frac{13}{9477} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nTaking the sixth root of both sides to solve for \\(r\\):\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\\[\nr = \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]\n\nNow we can find the value of \\(a\\) using \\(ar^2 = 1053\\):\n\\[\na \\left( \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}} \\right) = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na \\left( \\frac{13^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{9477^{\\frac{1}{3}}} \\right) = 1053\n\\]\n\nSimplifying \\(9477^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\):\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053 = 9 \\times (9 \\times 117) = 81 \\times 117\n\\]\n\\[\n9477^{\\frac{1}{3}} = (81 \\times 117)^{\\frac{1}{3}} = 9 \\times 117^{\\frac{1}{3}}\n\\]\n\\[\na \\left( \\frac{13^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{9 \\times 117^{\\frac{1}{3}}} \\right) = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na = 1053 \\left( \\frac{9 \\times 117^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{13^{\\frac{1}{3}}} \\right)\n\\]\n\nTo find the seventh term \\(a_7\\):\n\\[\na_7 = ar^6 = 1053 \\left( \\frac{9 \\times 117^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{13^{\\frac{1}{3}}} \\right) \\left( \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right)^1 \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\na_7 = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right)\n\\]\n\nFinally:\n\\[\na_7 = 39\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{39}"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{39}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Continuing from the interruption:\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{13}{9477}\\), we note that:\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053 = 9 \\times (9 \\times 117) = 81 \\times 117\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\frac{13}{9477} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nTaking the sixth root of both sides to solve for \\(r\\):\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\\[\nr = \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]\n\nNow we can find the value of \\(a\\) using \\(ar^2 = 1053\\):\n\\[\na \\left( \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}} \\right) = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na \\left( \\frac{13^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{9477^{\\frac{1}{3}}} \\right) = 1053\n\\]\n\nSimplifying \\(9477^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\):\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053 = 9 \\times (9 \\times 117) = 81 \\times 117\n\\]\n\\[\n9477^{\\frac{1}{3}} = (81 \\times 117)^{\\frac{1}{3}} = 9 \\times 117^{\\frac{1}{3}}\n\\]\n\\[\na \\left( \\frac{13^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{9 \\times 117^{\\frac{1}{3}}} \\right) = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na = 1053 \\left( \\frac{9 \\times 117^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{13^{\\frac{1}{3}}} \\right)\n\\]\n\nTo find the seventh term \\(a_7\\):\n\\[\na_7 = ar^6 = 1053 \\left( \\frac{9 \\times 117^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{13^{\\frac{1}{3}}} \\right) \\left( \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right)^1 \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\na_7 = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right) = 1053 \\left( \\frac{13}{9477} \\right)\n\\]\n\nFinally:\n\\[\na_7 = 39\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{39}","id":"algebra:753"}
